# Generated Chapter 5 Data File
CHAPTER_META = {'chapter_num': 5, 'title': 'Our Vibrant Country', 'class_level': 'Class 5', 'subject': 'Science (Curiosity, NEP 2020)', 'description': 'NCERT Class 5 Science Chapter 5: Our Vibrant Country interactive animated laboratory.'}

SLIDES = [{'id': 1, 'title': 'Republic Day', 'subtitle': 'National Pride, Tricolour Unfurling & The Grand New Delhi Parade', 'concept_html': '\n            <p>Every year on <strong>January 26th</strong>, our entire nation joins together in celebration of <strong>Republic Day</strong>. Schools across cities, towns, and villages begin the morning with students gathering around the central flagpole to witness the majestic unfurling of the Indian Tricolour flag (Tiranga), showered with rose petals as it rises to the breeze.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-2.5 my-3 text-xs">\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-orange-950/40 border border-orange-500/30">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-orange-400 block mb-1">Saffron (Kesari)</span>\n                    <span>Top band representing supreme courage, sacrifice, and the strength of our people.</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-slate-200 block mb-1">White & Navy Chakra</span>\n                    <span>Middle band of peace and truth, bearing the 24-spoke Ashoka Chakra denoting continuous righteous progress.</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-500/30">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">Green (Hara)</span>\n                    <span>Bottom band symbolising fertility, growth, auspiciousness, and the rich agricultural bounty of India.</span>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p>As the flag unfurls, students stand in disciplined attention to sing the <strong>National Anthem</strong>, <em>Jana Gana Mana</em>, composed by Rabindranath Tagore. Its lyrics celebrate the grand geographic expanse of our motherland—tracing sacred rivers like the Ganga and Yamuna, mountain chains like the Himalayas and Vindhyas, and coastal ocean shores.</p>\n            <p>In New Delhi along Kartavya Path, the grand parade showcases the brave Indian Defence Forces, supersonic aircraft flypasts, and colourful state tableaux displaying the diverse arts, architectures, and cultural folk dances from every corner of India.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Good morning students! Today we embark on an inspiring journey across our homeland in Chapter 5: Our Vibrant Country. Every year on January 26th, India comes alive celebrating Republic Day. We gather around our school flagpole, watching our proud tricolour unfurl towards the blue sky as fragrant flower petals flutter down. We stand tall singing Jana Gana Mana, a poetic salute celebrating our great rivers like the Ganga and Yamuna, and majestic peaks like the Himalayas. In New Delhi, the grand Republic Day parade presents state tableaux celebrating our unmatched diversity, alongside our courageous defence forces.', 'lecture_script': "Explains Republic Day, the symbolism of the Tricolour bands and the 24-spoke Ashoka Chakra, singing the National Anthem referencing India's rivers and mountains, and the New Delhi parade.", 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What does the 24-spoke navy blue Ashoka Chakra at the centre of the Indian flag signify?', 'options': ['24 seasons of the agrarian calendar', 'Continuous righteous motion and 24 hours of purposeful national progress', '24 major languages spoken across India', '24 mountain ranges in the Himalayas'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'The 24 spokes of the navy blue Ashoka Chakra represent the 24 hours of the day, signifying that there is life in movement and death in stagnation; our nation must constantly advance in truth and righteousness.', 'study_insight': 'The Ashoka Chakra is adapted from the Lion Capital of Emperor Ashoka at Sarnath, dating back to the 3rd century BCE.'}, {'question': 'Which two majestic geographical features are celebrated within the lyrics of the Indian National Anthem?', 'options': ['Thar Desert and Rann of Kutch', 'Vindhyas, Himalayas, and rivers Yamuna and Ganga', 'Western Ghats and Sunderbans', 'Deccan Plateau and Nilgiri Hills'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': "Jana Gana Mana specifically glorifies India's geography with 'Uchchala-Jaladhi-Taranga, Vindhya-Himachala-Yamuna-Ganga', highlighting our eternal mountains and life-giving rivers.", 'study_insight': 'Singing the national anthem takes approximately 52 seconds and instills a shared sense of patriotic unity.'}]}, {'id': 2, 'title': 'The Constitution', 'subtitle': 'The Supreme Law of the Land, Democratic Values & Peaceful Governance', 'concept_html': '\n            <p>While India attained independence from colonial rule on <strong>August 15, 1947</strong>, our country required its own foundational book of supreme laws to safeguard citizen rights, ensure justice, and guide democratic governance. On <strong>January 26, 1950</strong>, the <strong>Constitution of India</strong> came into full legal effect.</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-indigo-950/40 border border-indigo-500/30 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-indigo-400 block mb-1">The Architecture of Our Supreme Rulebook</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n                    Prepared under the leadership of <strong>Dr. Bhimrao Ramji Ambedkar</strong>, Chairman of the Drafting Committee, the Indian Constitution is the longest written national constitution in the world. It guarantees Fundamental Rights to all citizens regardless of religion, caste, gender, or birthplace.\n                </p>\n            </div>\n            <p>Just as a school playground needs clear game rules so that every child can play fairly and safely without bullies taking over, a massive nation of over 1.4 billion people needs a shared Constitution. It defines how laws are made by Parliament, enforced by the government, and protected by independent courts of law.</p>\n            <p>The Constitution also outlines our <strong>Fundamental Duties</strong>—reminding every child to protect public property, defend natural environments including forests and wildlife, strive towards excellence, and cherish our composite culture.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Did you know that gaining freedom on August 15, 1947 was only the first step? To ensure that every single citizen is treated with fairness and respect, India needed a supreme rulebook. That sacred book is the Constitution of India, adopted on January 26, 1950, which is why we celebrate Republic Day! Drafted under the visionary guidance of Dr. B. R. Ambedkar, our Constitution guarantees justice, equality, and liberty to all. Just as sports need strict rules so every student plays happily without bullying, our Constitution ensures peaceful harmony across our nation.', 'lecture_script': "Distinguishes Independence Day from Republic Day, explains why a nation needs a written Constitution, Dr. B.R. Ambedkar's role, and the balance of fundamental rights with duties.", 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Why is Republic Day celebrated specifically on January 26th rather than August 15th?', 'options': ["August 15th marks independence, while January 26, 1950 marks the adoption of India's Constitution", 'January 26th is the birth anniversary of Rabindranath Tagore', 'The Red Fort in Delhi was constructed on January 26th', 'India won its first hockey Olympic gold on that day'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Independence Day on August 15, 1947 celebrated freedom from foreign rule, whereas Republic Day on January 26, 1950 celebrated India establishing its own supreme democratic Constitution.', 'study_insight': 'January 26 was chosen because in 1930, the Indian National Congress had declared Purna Swaraj (complete self-rule) on that exact date.'}, {'question': 'Who served as the Chairman of the Drafting Committee that formulated the Constitution of India?', 'options': ['Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel', 'Dr. B. R. Ambedkar', 'Subhas Chandra Bose', 'Sarojini Naidu'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Dr. Bhimrao Ramji Ambedkar chaired the Drafting Committee, harmonising democratic principles to ensure social justice and fundamental equality for all Indian citizens.', 'study_insight': 'The original Constitution of India was handwritten in beautiful calligraphy by Prem Behari Narain Raizada, with exquisite illustrations by Nandalal Bose.'}]}, {'id': 3, 'title': 'Currency Secrets', 'subtitle': 'Micro-Details, Security Features & The Swachh Bharat Spectacles', 'concept_html': '\n            <p>Every Indian bank note—from ₹10 to ₹500—is not merely paper for buying supplies; it is an incredible canvas of Indian history, scientific engineering, and national ideals designed by the <strong>Reserve Bank of India (RBI)</strong>.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">🔍 Hidden Optical Watermark</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">\n                        When held up against sunlight or a lamp, a glowing translucent portrait of <strong>Mahatma Gandhi</strong> and the numerical denomination appear inside the blank white watermark window.\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">👓 Swachh Bharat Spectacles</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">\n                        Turn the note over! Beneath the central image, you will spot a pair of round spectacles with the words <em>\'Swachh\'</em> on the left lens and <em>\'Bharat\'</em> on the right lens, promoting national cleanliness.\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p>Currency notes also feature historical monuments that celebrate India\'s architectural and cultural glory: the Konark Sun Temple Wheel on the ₹10 note, the Ellora Caves on the ₹20 note, Hampi Stone Chariot on the ₹50 note, Rani ki Vav stepwell on the ₹100 note, Sanchi Stupa on the ₹200 note, and Delhi\'s Red Fort on the ₹500 note.</p>\n            <p>Across the back of each note is the <strong>Language Panel</strong>, printing the value in 15 distinct official Indian regional languages in addition to English and Hindi on the front, demonstrating linguistic inclusion.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': "Take a close look at an Indian currency note, like a ten-rupee or five-hundred-rupee note. It is packed with fascinating scientific and patriotic secrets! If you hold it up towards the sunlight, an invisible watermark of Mahatma Gandhi smiles back at you. Flip the note around, and you will spot a small pair of round spectacles. These glasses carry the slogan of the Swachh Bharat Mission, reminding us that cleanliness is a national service. You will also discover famous Indian monuments, and a language panel proudly displaying the note's value in fifteen different regional scripts!", 'lecture_script': 'Walks through security features on Indian currency notes: Mahatma Gandhi watermark, Swachh Bharat Abhiyan spectacles logo, architectural monuments, and the 15-language panel.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What national mission is represented on the reverse of modern Indian currency notes by a pair of round spectacles?', 'options': ['Digital India Mission', 'Swachh Bharat Abhiyan (Clean India Mission)', 'Make in India', 'National Solar Mission'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': "The round spectacles of Mahatma Gandhi with 'Swachh Bharat' printed inside the lenses represent the nationwide Swachh Bharat Abhiyan, urging citizens to maintain clean surroundings.", 'study_insight': 'Gandhiji believed that sanitation and cleanliness are just as vital as political independence.'}, {'question': 'How many regional languages are printed within the language panel on the reverse of an Indian rupee banknote?', 'options': ['5 languages', '10 languages', '15 languages', '28 languages'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'The language panel on the reverse displays the denomination in 15 regional Indian languages, in addition to Hindi and English displayed on the front of the note.', 'study_insight': 'The languages appear in alphabetical order according to the English spelling of each language name.'}]}, {'id': 4, 'title': 'Accessible Money', 'subtitle': 'Intaglio Tactile Markings, Bleed Lines & Assistive MANI Technology', 'concept_html': '\n            <p>How does a visually impaired person identify whether a bill in their hand is a ₹20, ₹100, or ₹500 note without having to ask someone else? Indian currency notes incorporate ingenious <strong>universal accessibility design</strong>.</p>\n            <div class="space-y-2.5 my-3 text-xs">\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/70 flex items-start space-x-2.5">\n                    <span class="text-base">🖐️</span>\n                    <div>\n                        <strong class="text-emerald-400">Raised Intaglio Printing:</strong>\n                        <span class="text-slate-300"> The portrait of Mahatma Gandhi, the Reserve Bank seal, and the denomination numerals are printed using raised relief ink that can be instantly felt by fingertips.</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/70 flex items-start space-x-2.5">\n                    <span class="text-base">📐</span>\n                    <div>\n                        <strong class="text-indigo-400">Raised Angular Bleed Lines & Shapes:</strong>\n                        <span class="text-slate-300"> Higher denomination notes feature raised diagonal lines along the edges: 4 bleed lines on ₹100 with a triangle, 4 lines with 2 circles on ₹200, and 5 bleed lines with a circle on ₹500.</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/70 flex items-start space-x-2.5">\n                    <span class="text-base">📱</span>\n                    <div>\n                        <strong class="text-amber-400">MANI Assistive Mobile App:</strong>\n                        <span class="text-slate-300"> The Reserve Bank of India developed the <em>Mobile Aided Note Identifier (MANI)</em> app. By holding a smartphone camera over any note, the app instantly identifies the denomination offline and speaks it aloud through audio or vibration cues.</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p>In addition, each denomination increases progressively in physical length and width, providing natural tactile size differentiation when stacked together in a wallet.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': "Have you ever wondered how people with visual challenges know the value of a currency note? India's currency includes wonderful accessibility features! Using special raised intaglio printing, the portrait of Mahatma Gandhi and distinct geometric marks can be felt with your fingertips. For example, the five-hundred-rupee note features five raised angular bleed lines on each border. Moreover, the Reserve Bank created a smart mobile app called MANI—Mobile Aided Note Identifier—which scans the note with a smartphone camera and speaks the value aloud, ensuring financial independence for everyone.", 'lecture_script': "Explains universal design in Indian currency: raised intaglio tactile printing, angular bleed lines, size differentiation, and the RBI's MANI assistive smartphone app.", 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the primary function of the raised angular bleed lines along the edges of higher-value banknotes?', 'options': ['To prevent the note from tearing at the corners', 'To allow visually impaired people to identify denominations by touch', 'To show where the note should be folded', 'To indicate the year the note was printed'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Raised angular bleed lines and geometric shapes printed in intaglio ink enable visually impaired citizens to discern the exact note denomination through tactile fingertip touch.', 'study_insight': 'Different denominations feature different counts of bleed lines: ₹100 has 4 lines, ₹200 has 4 with circles, and ₹500 has 5 lines.'}, {'question': 'What is the full name of the assistive mobile app developed by the RBI to help people identify currency denominations?', 'options': ['BHIM (Bharat Interface for Money)', 'MANI (Mobile Aided Note Identifier)', 'UMANG (Unified Mobile Application)', 'AADHAAR Financial Scanner'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'MANI stands for Mobile Aided Note Identifier. It scans currency notes using phone cameras even when offline and announces the denomination via voice audio or haptic vibration.', 'study_insight': 'MANI does not verify whether a currency note is authentic or counterfeit; it purely identifies denomination.'}]}, {'id': 5, 'title': 'National Emblem', 'subtitle': 'The Sarnath Lion Capital, Satyameva Jayate & The Gir Lion Miracle', 'concept_html': '\n            <p>The <strong>State Emblem of India</strong> is an adaptation of the ancient sandstone <strong>Lion Capital of Ashoka</strong>, carved around 250 BCE at Sarnath, Uttar Pradesh, where Gautama Buddha first taught his message of peace and compassion.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">🏛️ Anatomy of the Emblem</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">\n                        Four Asiatic lions stand back-to-back mounted on a circular abacus (three are visible in 2D views, while the fourth is hidden behind). Below them is the Ashoka Chakra flanked by a galloping horse on the left (energy and speed) and a humped bull on the right (steadfast hard work).\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">📜 Satyameva Jayate</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">\n                        Inscribed below the base in Devanagari script is our sacred national motto: <em>\'सत्यमेव जयते\'</em> (<strong>Truth Alone Triumphs</strong>), drawn from the ancient <em>Mundaka Upanishad</em>.\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p>The lions symbolise courage, power, and guardianship. In the wild, India is home to the world\'s only surviving population of <strong>Asiatic Lions</strong> in the dry deciduous forests of <strong>Gir National Park, Gujarat</strong>. Once reduced to barely 20 animals due to hunting a century ago, rigorous community-led conservation has revived their numbers to over 670 majestic lions today!</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': "Look closely at any official government document, coin, or passport, and you will see our National Emblem! It is adapted from the great Lion Capital erected by Emperor Ashoka over two thousand two hundred years ago at Sarnath. Four majestic Asiatic lions stand back-to-back, symbolising courage and vigilance, though in drawings we only see three. Underneath, our national motto proclaims in Devanagari: Satyameva Jayate, meaning Truth Alone Triumphs. India is also blessed to be the exclusive home of wild Asiatic lions in Gujarat's Gir forest, protected through proud conservation efforts.", 'lecture_script': 'Explains the State Emblem of India adapted from Sarnath Lion Capital, the four lions, the horse and bull, the motto Satyameva Jayate, and Asiatic lion conservation in Gir, Gujarat.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': "What is the meaning of the national motto 'Satyameva Jayate' inscribed below the National Emblem?", 'options': ['Unity is Our Strength', 'Truth Alone Triumphs', 'Peace Above All', 'Knowledge is Immortal'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': "'Satyameva Jayate' is a Sanskrit phrase from the Mundaka Upanishad that translates to 'Truth Alone Triumphs', reminding citizens and leaders that justice and truth always prevail.", 'study_insight': 'The motto was chosen by national leaders including Pandit Madan Mohan Malaviya and adopted on January 26, 1950.'}, {'question': 'Where is the only natural wild habitat of the Asiatic Lion located in India?', 'options': ['Kaziranga National Park, Assam', 'Gir Forest National Park, Gujarat', 'Jim Corbett National Park, Uttarakhand', 'Sundarbans Mangrove Forest, West Bengal'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'The Gir National Park and Wildlife Sanctuary in Gujarat is the only place on Earth where the Asiatic lion (Panthera leo persica) thrives in the wild.', 'study_insight': 'Asiatic lions have a distinct longitudinal fold of skin running along their belly, a feature rarely seen in African lions.'}]}, {'id': 6, 'title': 'The Tiger', 'subtitle': 'Apex Forest Predator, Natural Camouflage & Project Tiger', 'concept_html': '\n            <p>The majestic <strong>Royal Bengal Tiger</strong> (<em>Panthera tigris</em>) was declared the <strong>National Animal of India</strong> in 1973. Renowned for its grace, strength, and agility, the tiger reigns as the apex predator across India\'s varied ecosystems—from the snowy foothills of Uttarakhand to the tidal mangrove swamps of the Sundarbans.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-orange-950/40 border border-orange-500/30">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-orange-400 block mb-1">🌿 Striped Camouflage & Biology</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">\n                        No two tigers possess the exact same pattern of vertical dark stripes! In the dappled sunlight of grasslands and forests, these stripes break up the tiger\'s outline, making it practically invisible to prey like spotted deer and wild boar.\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-500/30">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">🛡️ Project Tiger Success Story</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">\n                        Launched in 1973 at Corbett National Park, <strong>Project Tiger</strong> established dedicated core sanctuaries free from human interference. Today, India is home to more than 75% of the world\'s wild tigers (over 3,600 tigers)!\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p>Tigers are known as an <strong>umbrella species</strong>. When we protect one tiger, we automatically protect its entire territory—thousands of hectares of lush forests, life-giving river watersheds, and hundreds of species of birds, insects, and deer.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': "With its glowing amber coat and dramatic black stripes, the Royal Bengal Tiger is India's National Animal. A tiger's stripes are as unique as human fingerprints; no two tigers on Earth have identical patterns! In tall grass, these stripes camouflage the tiger completely, enabling it to stalk silently on padded paws. Through India's pioneering Project Tiger launched in 1973, we turned the tide against poaching and habitat loss. Today, India proudly protects over three-quarters of the entire planet's wild tiger population, preserving forest biodiversity across the subcontinent.", 'lecture_script': "Explains the Royal Bengal Tiger as India's National Animal, unique stripe patterns, predatory camouflage, the umbrella species concept, and Project Tiger's success.", 'checkpoints': [{'question': "Why is the tiger described as an 'umbrella species' by wildlife biologists?", 'options': ['Because tigers use forest leaf canopies as shelter from heavy monsoon rains', "Because conserving the tiger's habitat automatically protects all other plants, rivers, and animals within that forest", 'Because tigers hunt animals that destroy shade trees', 'Because tigers have wide protective paws shaped like umbrellas'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': "An umbrella species requires a vast, healthy ecosystem to survive. By conserving large tiger reserves, we simultaneously safeguard rivers, forests, herbivores, insects, and birds living under that 'umbrella'.", 'study_insight': "A single adult tiger's territory can span between 20 to 100 square kilometres depending on prey availability."}, {'question': "What is unique about the dark vertical stripe pattern found on a tiger's coat?", 'options': ['The stripes disappear during winter months', 'Every tiger has an entirely unique stripe pattern, just like human fingerprints', 'Male and female tigers have identical stripe counts', 'The stripes are painted by other tigers using tree sap'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Every tiger has a completely unique stripe arrangement that remains on its skin even if shaved. Forest rangers use camera-trap stripe matching to identify individual tigers.', 'study_insight': "A tiger's roar can be heard echoing through the forest up to 3 kilometres away!"}]}, {'id': 7, 'title': 'The Peacock', 'subtitle': 'Iridescent Photonic Feathers, Cultural Lore & The Monsoon Dance', 'concept_html': '\n            <p>Declared the <strong>National Bird of India</strong> in 1963, the <strong>Indian Peafowl</strong> (<em>Pavo cristatus</em>) is celebrated for its unmatched elegance, rich cultural mythology, and close connection with the arrival of life-giving monsoon rains.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-blue-950/40 border border-blue-500/30">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-1">🪶 The Science of Iridescence</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">\n                        The brilliant emerald greens and shimmering sapphire blues of the peacock\'s long train are not caused by chemical dye pigments! Instead, microscopic two-dimensional photonic crystal structures on the feather barbules reflect light at specific wavelengths, shifting hues as the bird moves.\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-indigo-950/40 border border-indigo-500/30">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-indigo-400 block mb-1">🌧️ The Joyous Rain Dance</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">\n                        When heavy monsoon clouds gather and cool moist winds blow across parched plains, male peacocks fan out their magnificent iridescent trains into a giant circular disc, quivering their feathers with a distinct rustling sound to attract peahens.\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p>Peacocks are deeply woven into Indian heritage—adorning the crown of Lord Krishna, depicted in classical temple sculptures, and inspiring exquisite classical dance postures such as the <em>Mayura Mudra</em>.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Now turn your gaze to the skies and forest clearings for our National Bird: the Indian Peafowl, or Peacock! Its glittering blue neck and magnificent fanned tail have captivated Indian poets, dancers, and artists for thousands of years. But here is an astonishing scientific fact: the glowing blues and greens of its feathers are not made of coloured ink or pigment! They are created by tiny microscopic crystals on the feather surface that reflect light like prisms. When dark monsoon clouds roll in, the peacock fans its tail into a giant shimmering halo, dancing in celebration of the coming rain.', 'lecture_script': 'Explains the Indian Peafowl as the National Bird, the physics of feather iridescence, the courtship monsoon dance, and its cultural presence in classical dance mudras.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': "What scientific physical property produces the vivid shifting blue and green colours of a peacock's train feathers?", 'options': ['Deep blue chemical ink absorbed from berry seeds', 'Microscopic photonic crystalline structures that bend and reflect light waves (structural colouration)', 'Algae growing on the surface of the feathers', 'Bioluminescent bacteria glowing under moonlight'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Peacock feathers exhibit structural colouration: nanometre-scale keratin and melanin structures on the feather barbules interfere with light waves to produce vibrant, shifting iridescent colours without chemical pigments.', 'study_insight': 'Only male birds (peacocks) possess the long ornamental eye-spotted train; females (peahens) are mottled brown for nest camouflage.'}, {'question': 'Which season is traditionally associated with the famous circular fanned dance of the Indian peacock?', 'options': ['Peak dry summer in May', 'Arrival of the monsoon rains with dark cloudy skies', 'Harsh winter frost in December', 'Post-harvest autumn festivals'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Peacocks display their spectacular fanned courtship dance during the monsoon season, when cloudy skies, humidity, and thunder trigger energetic displays.', 'study_insight': 'In classical Indian dance traditions like Bharatanatyam and Kathak, dancers use the Mayura Mudra (hand gesture) to signify the peacock.'}]}, {'id': 8, 'title': 'Sacred Symbols', 'subtitle': 'The Banyan Tree, Lotus, River Dolphin & Elephant Heritage', 'concept_html': '\n            <p>Beyond the tiger and peacock, India\'s national emblems encompass sacred botanical wonders, aquatic marvels, and gentle forest giants that reflect our deep reverence for nature.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2.5 my-3 text-xs">\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-0.5">🌳 National Tree: Banyan (Kalpavriksha)</span>\n                    <span class="text-slate-300"><em>Ficus benghalensis</em> sends down thick aerial prop roots from its horizontal branches, creating sprawling, pillar-like canopies that shelter whole villages and live for centuries.</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-pink-400 block mb-0.5">🪷 National Flower: Lotus (Kamal)</span>\n                    <span class="text-slate-300"><em>Nelumbo nucifera</em> roots in muddy pond bottoms yet emerges with clean, pristine petals. Its leaves have a microscopic waxy texture that repels water droplets—the famous <em>Lotus Effect</em>.</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-cyan-400 block mb-0.5">🐬 National Aquatic Animal: Ganges River Dolphin</span>\n                    <span class="text-slate-300">The <em>Susu</em> (<em>Platanista gangetica</em>) is virtually blind in murky river silt; it navigates and hunts fish using high-frequency ultrasonic echolocation clicks.</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-0.5">🐘 National Heritage Animal: Indian Elephant</span>\n                    <span class="text-slate-300"><em>Elephas maximus</em> shapes forest corridors, disperses seeds across vast distances, and holds a sacred position in Indian folklore and festival traditions.</span>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p>Each of these living treasures reminds us that ancient India recognized ecological balance as an essential sacred duty centuries before modern conservation science arose.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Our vibrant national symbols extend far into the plant and animal kingdoms! The majestic Banyan tree, our National Tree, spreads its massive branches and drops aerial roots down into the soil, creating giant shady sanctuaries that endure for hundreds of years. The pink Lotus, our National Flower, blossoms untainted above muddy waters, showcasing its super-clean water-repellent leaves. In our sacred rivers swims the Ganges River Dolphin, our National Aquatic Animal, navigating murky waters using ultrasonic echolocation clicks. And roaming our dense forests is the Indian Elephant, our National Heritage Animal, revered as a wise guardian of biodiversity.', 'lecture_script': "Presents India's national natural heritage: Banyan tree (aerial prop roots), Lotus (Nelumbo nucifera and lotus effect), Ganges River Dolphin (echolocation), and Indian Elephant.", 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How does the Ganges River Dolphin navigate and locate fish in turbid, muddy river waters?', 'options': ['Using powerful night-vision eyes with glowing retinas', 'Using ultrasonic echolocation clicks that bounce off objects in the water', 'By detecting electric fields using long whiskers', 'By swimming only along the sunny surface of the river'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': "Because the silt-heavy waters of the Ganga and Brahmaputra are murky, the Ganges River Dolphin has rudimentary eyes without lenses; it emits ultrasonic echolocation clicks to 'see' its aquatic surroundings through sound echoes.", 'study_insight': "The dolphin surfaces every 30 to 120 seconds to breathe air, making a characteristic puffing sound called 'Susu'."}, {'question': 'What biological adaptation allows the Banyan tree (Ficus benghalensis) to expand outward over massive land areas?', 'options': ['Stems that burrow horizontally through deep bedrock', 'Aerial prop roots growing downward from horizontal branches that root into the earth as secondary trunks', 'Seeds that explode outward like mini-fireworks', 'Leaves that float across ponds to sprout new clones'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'The Banyan produces aerial roots from horizontal limbs. Upon reaching the ground, they thicken into stout woody pillars, supporting heavy branches and allowing the tree to spread over vast areas.', 'study_insight': 'The Great Banyan Tree in Howrah, West Bengal, has a canopy circumference of nearly half a kilometre and over 3,000 aerial roots!'}]}, {'id': 9, 'title': 'Many Languages', 'subtitle': 'A Thousand Living Tongues, Diverse Scripts & Eighth Schedule Heritage', 'concept_html': '\n            <p>One of the most extraordinary wonders of India is our linguistic richness. Over <strong>one thousand distinct mother tongues and dialects</strong> are spoken across the length and breadth of our country, each carrying centuries of poetry, folklore, lullabies, and scientific wisdom.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">📜 The Eighth Schedule</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">\n                        The Constitution of India formally recognizes <strong>22 official languages</strong> in the Eighth Schedule, including Assamese, Bengali, Gujarati, Hindi, Kannada, Kashmiri, Malayalam, Marathi, Odia, Punjabi, Sanskrit, Tamil, Telugu, and Urdu.\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">🔤 Ancient Scripts & Phonetics</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">\n                        Most Indian scripts—including Devanagari, Bengali, Gurmukhi, and Tamil—evolved from the ancient <strong>Brahmi script</strong>. Their phonetic systems are scientifically organized by place of articulation (throat, palate, teeth, lips).\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p>Traveling just 100 kilometres in India often brings a new dialect, new idioms, and new greeting customs. Despite having different alphabets and pronunciations, Indian languages share common cultural roots, philosophical concepts, and deep emotional warmth.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Imagine stepping into a magical library where over one thousand different tongues are spoken! That living library is India. Across our states, people speak hundreds of regional languages and dialects, each blessed with enchanting songs, bedtime tales, and profound literature. The Constitution of India recognizes twenty-two official languages in its Eighth Schedule. What is fascinating is that most Indian writing systems descend from the ancient Brahmi script, scientifically arranging sounds from the back of the throat to the tip of your lips. While our words may sound different, they speak the same universal message of love and fellowship.', 'lecture_script': "Explains India's linguistic diversity, the 22 languages recognized in the Eighth Schedule, the evolution of Indian scripts from Brahmi, and the scientific phonetic organization of Indian alphabets.", 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How many official regional languages are recognized under the Eighth Schedule of the Indian Constitution?', 'options': ['12 languages', '18 languages', '22 languages', '36 languages'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'The Eighth Schedule of the Constitution currently recognizes 22 official languages, providing each with institutional support and cultural dignity.', 'study_insight': 'English and Hindi are the official languages used for union government administrative communication.'}, {'question': 'From which ancient mother script did most modern Indian writing systems (like Devanagari, Bengali, and Tamil) evolve?', 'options': ['Egyptian Hieroglyphics', 'Ancient Brahmi Script', 'Roman Alphabet', 'Cuneiform Script'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': "The Brahmi script, visible on Emperor Ashoka's rock pillars from the 3rd century BCE, is the ancestral source of almost all modern Indian writing scripts.", 'study_insight': 'Indian alphabets are arranged scientifically: vowels first, followed by consonants classified by tongue contact points.'}]}, {'id': 10, 'title': 'Forest Metaphor', 'subtitle': 'Ecosystem Resilience: Why Diversity Makes India Unbreakable', 'concept_html': '\n            <p>Why is diversity a country\'s greatest strength rather than a weakness? In science, we can understand human society by observing the contrast between a <strong>man-made garden</strong> and a <strong>natural forest ecosystem</strong>.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">🏡 The Monoculture Garden</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">\n                        A lawn or rose garden features only one or two plant varieties. It requires artificial watering, chemical fertilizers, and constant weed removal. If a single fungus or insect pest attacks, the entire garden can wither and perish overnight.\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">🌲 The Self-Sustaining Forest</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">\n                        A virgin forest thrives without human gardeners! Tall teak trees, thorny shrubs, climbing vines, deep mosses, predatory birds, earthworms, and soil fungi form a robust web. If one species suffers, other species step in, maintaining total balance.\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p>India is like a vast ancient forest. If everyone wore identical clothes, ate the exact same meal, and spoke identical phrases, our society would be brittle and uniform. Our multiplicity of languages, cuisines, festival calendars, and skills creates an unbreakable, resilient national fabric!</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Let us explore a profound science lesson from nature: the power of diversity! Think about a manicured garden with only one type of rose. It looks neat, but it needs constant care. If a single disease strikes, every bush dies because they are all identical. Now look at a wild natural forest! A forest contains thousands of trees, birds, insects, and creepers. It needs no watering cans or fertilizers because its diversity makes it self-healing and indestructible. India is just like that grand forest! Our diversity of customs, crafts, and ideas makes our nation resilient, vibrant, and strong.', 'lecture_script': 'Compares an artificial monoculture garden to a biodiverse forest ecosystem to explain why cultural and social diversity creates strength and adaptability for India.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'In ecological science, why is a diverse natural forest far more resilient to diseases and droughts than a monoculture lawn?', 'options': ['Forest trees have deeper roots that drink up all rainwater instantly', 'Multiple diverse species support mutual nutrient cycling, natural pest control, and ecological redundancy', 'Forest animals chase away storms and hail', 'Monoculture lawns grow faster than forest trees'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Ecological diversity ensures that if one plant species suffers an infection or drought stress, hundreds of other companion species sustain the food web and soil health, keeping the entire forest alive.', 'study_insight': 'In society, cognitive and cultural diversity provides varied perspectives to solve complex problems innovatively.'}, {'question': "How does the 'forest versus garden' comparison explain India's national strength?", 'options': ['India should cut down gardens to plant more fruit trees', 'Our wide variety of traditions, languages, and skills makes the nation resilient and adaptable like a forest', 'Everyone in India must live in the forest', 'Gardens are weaker because they have no fences'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': "Just like a forest thrives on varied plant and animal species, India's plural traditions and mutual community support make our society resilient and unshakeable.", 'study_insight': 'Ecologist Charles Elton proved in 1958 that diverse natural communities are more stable than simple, uniform communities.'}]}, {'id': 11, 'title': 'Regional Headgear', 'subtitle': 'Saafas, Topis, Turbans & Functional Modern Safety Helmets', 'concept_html': '\n            <p>From the arid dunes of the Thar Desert to the snowy valleys of the Himalayas, traditional Indian headgear represents local climate adaptations, festive honour, and artistic identity.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-2.5 my-3 text-xs">\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-orange-950/40 border border-orange-500/30">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-orange-400 block mb-1">🏜️ Rajasthani Saafa / Pagri</span>\n                    <span>Multi-metre bright cotton turban wrapped with dozens of airy folds. It shields the head from scorching desert sun and traps cool morning air inside the insulating fabric layers.</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-sky-950/40 border border-sky-500/30">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-1">🏔️ Himachali & Kullu Topi</span>\n                    <span>Sturdy woollen cap adorned with vibrant handwoven geometric patterned borders. Designed to insulate the scalp and ears from frigid mountain frost and icy winds.</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-500/30">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">👑 Mysore Peta & Maharashtrian Pheta</span>\n                    <span>Aristocratic silk and zari turbans worn during weddings, formal celebrations, and state convocations as a mark of deep respect, scholarship, and festive dignity.</span>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p>Alongside traditional heritage turbans, modern India embraces <strong>functional safety headgear</strong>: tough shock-absorbing polycarbonate helmets worn by construction engineers, factory workers, and two-wheeler motorists to protect human brains from impact injuries.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Look at the colorful headgear worn across India! In the scorching deserts of Rajasthan, men wear the voluminous Saafa or Pagri—long strips of cotton wrapped in airy folds that insulate the skull from burning sunbeams. High in the cold Himalayan ranges of Himachal Pradesh, people wear warm woollen topis with intricate embroidered bands to lock in body heat. In Maharashtra and Karnataka, the regal Pheta and Mysore Peta represent royal honour and academic excellence. And on modern roads and work sites, citizens wear sturdy safety helmets to protect the fragile human skull, uniting tradition with safety science.', 'lecture_script': 'Explores traditional headgear across India: Rajasthani Saafa (thermal desert insulation), Himachali woollen Topi, Mysore Peta, and the life-saving physics of modern safety helmets.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What scientific climatic function does the multi-layered cotton Saafa or Pagri serve for desert dwellers in Rajasthan?', 'options': ['It acts as a radio antenna to catch regional songs', 'It traps layers of air within the loose cotton folds, providing thermal insulation against scorching solar heat', 'It serves as a heavy weight to help walking during sandstorms', 'It glows in the dark to guide camels at night'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': "Air trapped between the multiple folds of lightweight cotton serves as a natural thermal insulator, keeping the wearer's skull cooler during hot desert days.", 'study_insight': "In ancient times, a traveler's long turban could also be unwound to draw water from deep desert wells or act as a blanket at night."}, {'question': 'Why is wearing an ISI-certified safety helmet compulsory for two-wheeler motorists and construction workers?', 'options': ['To prevent wind from messing up hair', 'To cushion deceleration force and protect the skull and brain from traumatic impact injury', 'To match the colour of the motorcycle', 'To block the sound of car horns'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': "Safety helmets have a hard outer shell that disperses impact energy and an inner EPS foam liner that crushes gradually, slowing down the head's deceleration and preventing deadly concussions.", 'study_insight': 'Wearing a proper helmet reduces the risk of fatal head injury by more than 70% in two-wheeler accidents.'}]}, {'id': 12, 'title': 'Rhythm Drums', 'subtitle': 'The Acoustic Science of Dhol, Mridangam, Tabla & Pakhawaj', 'concept_html': '\n            <p>From festival processions to classical music concerts, India pulses with extraordinary rhythmic energy. Our regional percussion instruments—known as <strong>Avanaddha Vadya</strong> (membranophones)—showcase ingenious acoustic physics.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">🥁 The Booming Dhol & Dholak</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">\n                        A large barrel-shaped wooden drum with two leather heads. The wider base head produces a deep, resonant bass thump that carries across vast outdoor agricultural harvest fairs like Baisakhi and Lohri.\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">🎶 Mridangam, Tabla & Pakhawaj</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">\n                        In classical Carnatic and Hindustani music, drum heads feature a central black circular patch called <strong>Syahi</strong> or <em>Karanai</em> (made of iron filings, soot, and starch). This extra mass tunes vibrating overtones into clear, harmonic musical notes!\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p>Unlike Western drums that often produce unpitched noise, Indian percussionists engineered membranes capable of producing exact musical pitches (tones) matching the tanpura drone!</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Listen to the beating heartbeat of India: our drums! From the thunderous Dhol of Punjab to the sweet rhythms of the Dholak, percussion instruments bring our festivals alive. In classical traditions, Indian musicians developed instruments like the Mridangam and Tabla. If you look at a Tabla head, you will notice a dark circular spot in the centre called the Syahi. Indian craftsmen discovered hundreds of years ago that applying a paste of iron filings and rice starch adds weight to the drum skin, dampening harsh clatter and turning the drum beat into melodious musical notes with perfect pitch!', 'lecture_script': 'Explores the acoustic physics of Indian drums: membranophone sound production, the deep bass resonance of Dhol, and the harmonic overtones created by the syahi patch on Tabla and Mridangam.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the scientific purpose of the dark circular patch (Syahi or Karanai) applied to the leather head of a Tabla or Mridangam?', 'options': ['To hide accidental cuts or holes in the animal hide', 'To weight the central membrane, eliminating dissonance and producing harmonically tuned musical overtones', 'To make the drum waterproof against rain', "To prevent the player's fingers from slipping"], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Physicist Sir C.V. Raman studied the Indian drum in 1920 and proved that the concentric black Syahi loading shifts non-harmonic circular drum modes into harmonic overtones, making drums pitch-tunable like string instruments.', 'study_insight': 'Sir C. V. Raman called the Mridangam and Tabla a triumph of Indian acoustic genius.'}, {'question': 'Which festival is famously associated with energetic outdoor dancing to the booming beats of the Dhol in northern India?', 'options': ['Baisakhi (Harvest celebration of Punjab)', 'Onam boat races', 'Hemis Festival in Ladakh', 'Rath Yatra in Puri'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Baisakhi is the joyous spring harvest festival of Punjab where farmers and communities perform the energetic Bhangra dance accompanied by the thunderous rhythm of the Dhol.', 'study_insight': "The dhol is played using two wooden sticks: a thicker curved 'dagga' for the bass and a thin flexible 'tihli' for the treble."}]}, {'id': 13, 'title': 'Classical Dances', 'subtitle': 'Geometrical Postures, Storytelling Mudras & Expressive Abhinaya', 'concept_html': '\n            <p>India is home to eight celebrated <strong>Classical Dance Forms</strong> recognized across different geographic regions, each originating from temple storytelling and rooted in the ancient Sanskrit treatise <em>Natya Shastra</em>.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2.5 my-3 text-xs">\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-orange-400 block mb-0.5">💃 Bharatanatyam (Tamil Nadu)</span>\n                    <span class="text-slate-300">Characterized by sculptural geometric lines, crisp stamping footwork (tattadavu), bent-knee plie postures (aramandi), and dynamic torso poise.</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-0.5">🌀 Kathak (Northern India)</span>\n                    <span class="text-slate-300">Renowned for lightning-fast pirouettes (chakkars), subtle facial mime (abhinaya), and rhythmic conversation between ghungroo ankle bells and the tabla.</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-0.5">🎭 Kathakali (Kerala)</span>\n                    <span class="text-slate-300">Vivid dance-drama featuring elaborate facial face-paint (chutti), monumental headdresses, and hyper-expressive ocular (eye) ball movements.</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-pink-400 block mb-0.5">🌊 Odissi (Odisha) & Manipuri (Manipur)</span>\n                    <span class="text-slate-300">Odissi captures temple sculpture stances with its lyrical <em>Tribhanga</em> (three-bend posture), while Manipuri glides with graceful, floating circular movements.</span>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p>Dancers communicate complex epics without uttering a word using <strong>Hastas or Mudras</strong> (codified hand gestures representing deer, lotuses, kings, and rivers) paired with <strong>Navarasas</strong> (the nine human emotions).</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Step into the magical realm of Indian classical dance! Our motherland has gifted the world eight splendid classical dance styles. In Tamil Nadu, Bharatanatyam features sharp geometric postures and lightning footwork. In the north, Kathak dazzles with spinning pirouettes and intricate rhythmic footwork matching the tabla. In Kerala, Kathakali artists paint their faces in vibrant greens and reds to enact epics through dramatic eye movements. In Odisha, Odissi dancers bend like living temple sculptures in the graceful three-part Tribhanga posture. Through graceful hand gestures called Mudras, dancers can depict a flying swan, a blossoming lotus, or gentle raindrops!', 'lecture_script': 'Introduces Indian classical dance forms: Bharatanatyam, Kathak, Kathakali, Odissi, Manipuri, the use of Mudras (hand gestures) and Navarasas (nine emotions) for storytelling.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the three-bend body posture characteristic of classical Odissi dance called?', 'options': ['Aramandi', 'Tribhanga (head, torso, and hips curved in opposite directions)', 'Chakkar', 'Tattadavu'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': "'Tribhanga' literally translates to 'three bends'. In Odissi, the body curves gracefully at the neck, waist, and knee, mimicking the immortal stone sculptures of Konark and Puri temples.", 'study_insight': "The opposite grounded stance in Odissi is called 'Chauka', a square posture representing Lord Jagannatha."}, {'question': 'What visual communication tool do classical Indian dancers use to depict animals, flowers, and natural elements?', 'options': ['Hand-drawn flashcards held up to the audience', 'Hastas or Mudras (codified symbolic hand and finger gestures)', 'Chalk drawings on the stage floor', 'Shadow puppet projections'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Mudras (or Hastas) are codified hand and finger gestures used in Indian classical dance to silently convey characters, animals (like deer and peacocks), water, and emotions.', 'study_insight': 'The Natya Shastra enumerates 28 single-hand gestures (Asamyutta Hastas) and 24 double-hand gestures (Samyutta Hastas).'}]}, {'id': 14, 'title': 'Ancient Wisdom', 'subtitle': 'Ayurvedic Phytotherapy, Yoga Physiology & Daily Wellness Science', 'concept_html': '\n            <p>Thousands of years before modern synthetic pharmaceuticals and fitness gyms, ancient Indian scholars developed systematic sciences of health, mind control, and preventative wellness: <strong>Ayurveda</strong> (the science of life) and <strong>Yoga</strong>.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">🌿 Botanical Phytochemistry</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">\n                        Household herbs long prescribed in Ayurveda are now verified by modern biochemistry: <strong>Haldi (Turmeric)</strong> contains <em>curcumin</em>, a powerful anti-inflammatory and antiseptic; <strong>Tulsi (Holy Basil)</strong> contains <em>eugenol</em> for respiratory immunity; and <strong>Neem</strong> provides potent antibacterial and antifungal protection.\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">🧘 Yoga & Pranayama Biology</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">\n                        Synthesised by Sage Patanjali, Yoga unites physical posture (Asana) with rhythmic breath regulation (Pranayama). Deep, slow breathing stimulates the parasympathetic nervous system, lowering heart rate, soothing anxiety, and boosting cerebral oxygen delivery.\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p>Today, the entire world joins India on <strong>June 21st</strong> to celebrate the <strong>International Day of Yoga</strong>, recognizing our ancient heritage as a universal gift for global health and mental peace.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Long before modern hospitals and synthetic medicines were invented, India pioneered holistic systems of health and balance: Ayurveda and Yoga! Think of the home remedies your elders share: golden turmeric milk when you have a sore throat, fresh Tulsi leaves for a cough, or bitter Neem for clean skin. Modern science has now proved that turmeric contains curcumin, a powerful germ-fighting molecule! Meanwhile, the science of Yoga and Pranayama trains our breathing and postures, calming the nervous system and sharpening memory. Today, millions of people worldwide celebrate the International Day of Yoga on June 21st!', 'lecture_script': 'Explains Ayurveda and household medicinal herbs (turmeric/curcumin, tulsi, neem), the biological benefits of Yoga and Pranayama on heart rate and oxygenation, and International Yoga Day on June 21st.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What bioactive compound found in golden turmeric (Haldi) gives it powerful anti-inflammatory and antiseptic properties?', 'options': ['Caffeine', 'Curcumin', 'Chlorophyll', 'Nicotine'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Curcumin is the active phytochemical in turmeric, proven by modern pharmacology to reduce cellular inflammation, accelerate wound healing, and fight bacterial infections.', 'study_insight': 'Turmeric combined with black pepper increases curcumin absorption in the human body by up to 2,000% due to piperine.'}, {'question': "On which date does the world celebrate the International Day of Yoga, established through India's initiative at the United Nations?", 'options': ['January 26th', 'June 21st (Summer Solstice)', 'August 15th', 'October 2nd'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'June 21st, the longest day of the year in the Northern Hemisphere (Summer Solstice), was designated as the International Day of Yoga by the UN General Assembly in 2014.', 'study_insight': "Yoga comes from the Sanskrit root 'Yuj', meaning to join or unite individual consciousness with universal awareness."}]}, {'id': 15, 'title': 'Modern Progress', 'subtitle': 'ISRO Space Exploration, Digital UPI & Vande Bharat High-Speed Rail', 'concept_html': '\n            <p>While deeply rooted in thousands of years of tradition, India simultaneously charges forward as a global leader in high-tech innovation, digital public infrastructure, and space exploration.</p>\n            <div class="space-y-2.5 my-3 text-xs">\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/70 flex items-start space-x-2.5">\n                    <span class="text-base">🚀</span>\n                    <div>\n                        <strong class="text-orange-400">ISRO Cosmic Triumphs:</strong>\n                        <span class="text-slate-300"> India made history with the <strong>Chandrayaan-3</strong> mission by becoming the first country on Earth to land a spacecraft near the challenging South Pole of the Moon! Alongside the <em>Aditya-L1</em> solar observatory, the Indian Space Research Organisation delivers world-class space science at a fraction of global costs.</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/70 flex items-start space-x-2.5">\n                    <span class="text-base">💳</span>\n                    <div>\n                        <strong class="text-cyan-400">UPI Digital Revolution:</strong>\n                        <span class="text-slate-300"> The <strong>Unified Payments Interface (UPI)</strong> allows roadside vegetable vendors, autorickshaw drivers, and large stores to receive instant, zero-cost payments using simple QR codes on mobile phones. India now powers over 45% of all real-time digital payments across the planet!</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/70 flex items-start space-x-2.5">\n                    <span class="text-base">🚅</span>\n                    <div>\n                        <strong class="text-emerald-400">Vande Bharat Express & Clean Energy:</strong>\n                        <span class="text-slate-300"> Indigenously designed semi-high-speed electric trains connect cities with aerodynamic energy efficiency, complemented by massive solar parks and wind energy grids driving green development.</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p>Modern technology in India does not erase our ancient heritage; it empowers citizens, connects distant villages, and carries our timeless values into the future.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': "Behold the breathtaking modern face of India! Our nation is taking giant leaps into the future. With the historic Chandrayaan-3 mission, the Indian Space Research Organisation, ISRO, made India the first nation in history to land a rover on the Moon's mysterious South Pole! Down on our streets, the Unified Payments Interface, or UPI, has transformed our economy. Even a coconut vendor on a cart accepts instant payments with a quick smartphone QR scan, making India the world leader in digital transactions. Add to that our supersonic Vande Bharat trains and massive solar parks, and you see how India builds the future while honouring her roots!", 'lecture_script': "Highlights India's modern achievements: ISRO's Chandrayaan-3 south pole lunar landing, Aditya-L1 solar probe, the UPI digital payment revolution, and aerodynamic Vande Bharat trains.", 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which historic global space milestone was achieved by the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO) with Chandrayaan-3 in 2023?', 'options': ['First crewed landing on Mars', 'First nation to achieve a soft touchdown near the lunar South Pole', 'First space probe to leave the solar system', 'First telescope built on an asteroid'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': "On August 23, 2023, ISRO's Vikram lander successfully touched down near the south pole of the Moon, making India the first nation in human history to land in this rugged, water-ice-rich polar region.", 'study_insight': 'August 23 is now commemorated annually in India as National Space Day.'}, {'question': "What is the primary benefit of the Unified Payments Interface (UPI) in India's daily economic life?", 'options': ['It charges high fees on all transactions', 'It enables instant, secure, real-time money transfers between bank accounts using simple phone QR codes', 'It replaces banks entirely with foreign servers', 'It works only in air-conditioned shopping malls'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Developed by the National Payments Corporation of India (NPCI), UPI enables real-time peer-to-peer and merchant digital transactions directly from smartphones via QR codes with zero friction.', 'study_insight': 'India processes billions of UPI transactions every month, exceeding the digital transaction volumes of many major economies combined.'}]}, {'id': 16, 'title': 'United Fabric', 'subtitle': 'Unity in Diversity: The Irreplaceable Strength of Our Composite Culture', 'concept_html': '\n            <p>Throughout our journey across Chapter 5, we have discovered that India is not a melting pot where all unique flavours vanish into one dull soup. Instead, India is a magnificent <strong>mosaic or rich tapestry</strong>, where every thread—every dialect, every folk dance, every festival, and every modern dream—retains its own brilliant colour while weaving together into one unbreakable fabric.</p>\n            <div class="p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-gradient-to-r from-orange-950/40 via-slate-900 to-emerald-950/40 border border-amber-500/30 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-300 block mb-1">🤝 The Principles of Unity in Diversity</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n                    Whether we celebrate Diwali, Eid, Christmas, Gurpurab, Baisakhi, Pongal, or Buddha Purnima, we share our sweets and greetings with neighbors. When our scientists launch a rocket or our athletes win Olympic medals, every Indian heart beats with the same swelling pride.\n                </p>\n            </div>\n            <p>Our founding leaders encapsulated this spirit in the historic phrase: <strong>\'Unity in Diversity\'</strong>. It reminds us that our differences are not barriers that divide us; they are the dazzling treasures that make India vibrant, compassionate, and enduring.</p>\n            <p>As responsible young citizens of this vibrant democracy, our duty is to celebrate each other\'s languages, protect our environment, uphold constitutional justice, and contribute our unique talents to national progress.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'As we conclude our conceptual journey, let us celebrate the grand truth of our motherland: Unity in Diversity! India is not like a melting pot where everything gets ground into the same dull shape. India is like a magnificent handwoven saree or grand carpet! Each strand of thread—whether it is a folk song from Assam, a temple dance from Tamil Nadu, a desert turban from Rajasthan, or a space rover built in Bengaluru—keeps its unique, sparkling brilliance while binding tightly into an invincible national fabric. When we respect each other, care for our environment, and stand shoulder-to-shoulder, our vibrant India shines bright across the world!', 'lecture_script': "Synthesizes the core philosophy of 'Unity in Diversity', composite cultural heritage, mutual respect across communities, and citizen duty toward a harmonious, progressive India.", 'checkpoints': [{'question': "What does the famous national phrase 'Unity in Diversity' truly mean in the context of Indian culture?", 'options': ['That everyone must abandon their native language and speak only one language', 'That our diverse regional languages, arts, and traditions unite into a harmonious, strong national fabric without erasing individuality', 'That people must wear identical clothes during festivals', 'That state borders should be permanently sealed'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': "'Unity in Diversity' describes a composite society where varied cultures, languages, and belief systems harmoniously coexist, finding common purpose, mutual respect, and national integration.", 'study_insight': "The phrase was popularized by Jawaharlal Nehru in his landmark book 'The Discovery of India'."}, {'question': 'What is the primary responsibility of young Indian citizens toward preserving this vibrant national fabric?', 'options': ['To only associate with people from their own state', 'To respect all cultures, protect nature, uphold constitutional values, and learn from one another', 'To avoid learning regional languages', 'To stop playing sports with students from other communities'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': "Active citizenship requires mutual respect across communities, protecting shared natural heritage, upholding constitutional rights and duties, and celebrating India's rich cultural diversity.", 'study_insight': 'Article 51A of the Indian Constitution enshrines the duty of every citizen to value and preserve the rich heritage of our composite culture.'}]}]

QUIZ_QUESTIONS = [{'id': 1, 'question': 'Which date marks the historic adoption of the Constitution of India, celebrated nationwide as Republic Day?', 'options': ['15 August 1947', '26 January 1950', '2 October 1869', '14 November 1889'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'India adopted its Constitution on 26 January 1950, transitioning into a sovereign democratic republic.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Step-by-Step Historical Analysis:</strong></p>\n                <ul class='list-disc pl-4 space-y-1 text-slate-300'>\n                    <li><strong>15 August 1947:</strong> India gained independence from British colonial rule (Independence Day).</li>\n                    <li><strong>26 January 1950:</strong> The Constitution of India came into full legal force, giving the nation its supreme democratic law (Republic Day).</li>\n                    <li><strong>2 October:</strong> Gandhi Jayanti (birth anniversary of Mahatma Gandhi).</li>\n                </ul>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (26 January 1950)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 2, 'question': 'What does the top saffron (Kesari) colour band of the Indian National Flag represent?', 'options': ['Wealth and gold mines', 'Courage, sacrifice, and the strength of the nation', 'Abundance of agricultural crops', 'Peace and silence'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Saffron represents courage, selfless sacrifice, and the indomitable strength of the people of India.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Tricolour Band Symbolism:</strong></p>\n                <ul class='list-disc pl-4 space-y-1 text-slate-300'>\n                    <li><strong class='text-orange-400'>Saffron (Top):</strong> Courage, valor, and sacrifice.</li>\n                    <li><strong class='text-slate-200'>White (Middle):</strong> Peace, truth, and purity with Dharma Chakra.</li>\n                    <li><strong class='text-emerald-400'>Green (Bottom):</strong> Prosperity, fertility, and auspiciousness of the soil.</li>\n                </ul>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Courage, sacrifice, and the strength of the nation)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 3, 'question': 'How many spokes are carved into the Ashoka Chakra situated at the centre of the Indian Flag?', 'options': ['12 spokes', '16 spokes', '24 spokes', '36 spokes'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'The Ashoka Chakra contains 24 navy blue spokes representing the 24 hours of the day and relentless righteous progress.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Ashoka Chakra Geometry & Meaning:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>The wheel of law is taken from the Sarnath Lion Capital of Emperor Ashoka. Its 24 spokes represent 24 righteous virtues and 24 hours of continuous purposeful motion: movement is life, stagnation is death.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: C (24 spokes)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 4, 'question': "Who composed India's National Anthem, 'Jana Gana Mana'?", 'options': ['Bankim Chandra Chatterjee', 'Rabindranath Tagore', 'Sarojini Naidu', 'Subhas Chandra Bose'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': "Rabindranath Tagore composed 'Jana Gana Mana' in 1911, officially adopted as India's National Anthem in January 1950.", 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Literary & Patriotic Origin:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Nobel laureate Rabindranath Tagore penned 'Jana Gana Mana' in highly Sanskritized Bengali. Bankim Chandra Chatterjee composed 'Vande Mataram' (the National Song).</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Rabindranath Tagore)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 5, 'question': 'Which of the following bodies of water is specifically glorified in the lyrics of the Indian National Anthem?', 'options': ['Arabian Sea', 'Yamuna and Ganga', 'Brahmaputra and Godavari', 'Chilika Lake'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': "The anthem contains the verse 'Vindhya-Himachala-Yamuna-Ganga', revering the sacred rivers Yamuna and Ganga.", 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Geographical Sweep in the Anthem:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>The stanza celebrates Punjab, Sindh, Gujarat, Maratha, Dravida, Utkala, and Vanga, alongside the Vindhya and Himachal mountain ranges and the life-giving Yamuna and Ganga rivers.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Yamuna and Ganga)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 6, 'question': 'Who served as the Chairman of the Drafting Committee of the Constitution of India?', 'options': ['Jawaharlal Nehru', 'Dr. B. R. Ambedkar', 'Dr. Rajendra Prasad', 'Maulana Abul Kalam Azad'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Dr. Bhimrao Ramji Ambedkar chaired the committee that drafted the supreme Constitution of India.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Constitutional Leadership:</strong></p>\n                <ul class='list-disc pl-4 space-y-1 text-slate-300'>\n                    <li><strong>Dr. B. R. Ambedkar:</strong> Chairman of the Drafting Committee ('Architect of the Indian Constitution').</li>\n                    <li><strong>Dr. Rajendra Prasad:</strong> President of the Constituent Assembly.</li>\n                    <li><strong>Jawaharlal Nehru:</strong> Prime Minister who moved the historic Objectives Resolution.</li>\n                </ul>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Dr. B. R. Ambedkar)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 7, 'question': 'What is the primary role of a Constitution in a democratic nation like India?', 'options': ['To tell shopkeepers what prices to charge for food', 'To provide a supreme framework of rules, guarantee fundamental rights, and balance government powers', 'To decide which sports teams win competitions', 'To choose which movies should be shown in theatres'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'A Constitution is the foundational legal document defining citizen rights, state obligations, and legal governance rules.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Purpose of the Constitution:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>The Constitution prevents authoritarian abuse of power, guarantees fundamental human rights (equality, freedom of speech, justice), and establishes fair checks between the Legislature, Executive, and Judiciary.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 8, 'question': 'Along which historic avenue in New Delhi is the Republic Day Parade conducted every year?', 'options': ['Marine Drive', 'Kartavya Path (formerly Rajpath)', 'Mall Road', 'Grand Trunk Road'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The Republic Day parade takes place along Kartavya Path from Rashtrapati Bhavan to India Gate in New Delhi.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Ceremonial Avenue of New Delhi:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Kartavya Path ('Path of Duty') serves as the ceremonial boulevard hosting the President, foreign heads of state, the Armed Forces, and cultural tableaux representing diverse states.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Kartavya Path)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 9, 'question': 'What is the normal duration required to sing the full version of the Indian National Anthem?', 'options': ['25 seconds', '52 seconds', '90 seconds', '120 seconds'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The standard official timing for playing or singing the full version of Jana Gana Mana is approximately 52 seconds.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Anthem Timing Code:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>By national protocol, the complete 5-stanza song's first formal verse is performed with dignified cadence in exactly 52 seconds (with an abbreviated version of the first and last lines taking about 20 seconds).</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (52 seconds)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 10, 'question': 'Which of the following is a Fundamental Duty of every Indian citizen according to the Constitution?', 'options': ['To watch the Republic Day parade on television', 'To protect and improve the natural environment, including forests, lakes, rivers, and wildlife', 'To speak at least five languages', 'To visit New Delhi once a year'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Article 51A(g) states it is the duty of every citizen to protect and improve forests, lakes, rivers, and wildlife and have compassion for living creatures.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Constitutional Ecological Duty:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Fundamental Duties remind citizens that rights come with responsibilities. Preserving natural environments and public property is essential for the nation's well-being.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 11, 'question': "Which national leader's portrait appears as a hidden translucent watermark on Indian currency notes?", 'options': ['Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel', 'Mahatma Gandhi', 'Subhas Chandra Bose', 'Dr. B. R. Ambedkar'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'A delicate portrait of Mahatma Gandhi appears in the translucent watermark window when held to light.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Watermark Security Technology:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>During paper manufacturing, paper thickness is varied in specific regions. When backlit by light, varying paper density reveals a three-dimensional grayscale portrait of Mahatma Gandhi and denomination figures.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Mahatma Gandhi)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 12, 'question': 'What is depicted inside the lenses of the round spectacles logo printed on the back of modern Indian banknotes?', 'options': ["'Satyameva' and 'Jayate'", "'Swachh' and 'Bharat'", "'Reserve' and 'Bank'", "'Clean' and 'Green'"], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': "The spectacles feature the words 'Swachh' on the left lens and 'Bharat' on the right lens, commemorating the Swachh Bharat Abhiyan.", 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Clean India Emblem on Currency:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Mahatma Gandhi's iconic wire-rimmed glasses serve as the official logo for Swachh Bharat Abhiyan, reminding every citizen exchanging currency of our shared duty toward cleanliness.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B ('Swachh' and 'Bharat')</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 13, 'question': 'Which historic architectural monument is illustrated on the reverse side of the Indian ₹10 banknote?', 'options': ['Red Fort, Delhi', 'Konark Sun Temple Stone Wheel, Odisha', 'Sanchi Stupa, Madhya Pradesh', 'Hampi Chariot, Karnataka'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The ₹10 note features the famous 24-spoke stone wheel from the 13th-century Konark Sun Temple in Odisha.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Currency Monument Matcher:</strong></p>\n                <ul class='list-disc pl-4 space-y-1 text-slate-300'>\n                    <li><strong>₹10:</strong> Konark Sun Temple Wheel (Odisha)</li>\n                    <li><strong>₹50:</strong> Hampi Stone Chariot (Karnataka)</li>\n                    <li><strong>₹100:</strong> Rani ki Vav Stepwell (Gujarat)</li>\n                    <li><strong>₹500:</strong> Red Fort (New Delhi)</li>\n                </ul>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Konark Sun Temple Stone Wheel, Odisha)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 14, 'question': 'What printing technique gives the portrait of Mahatma Gandhi on banknotes a raised relief texture felt by fingertips?', 'options': ['Lithography printing', 'Intaglio printing', 'Screen printing', 'Dot matrix stamping'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Intaglio printing transfers thick viscous ink under immense mechanical pressure, leaving raised tactile ridges that can be easily felt by touch.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Science of Intaglio Printing:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Intaglio plates have engraved recessed grooves that hold heavy ink. When paper is pressed against the plate at thousands of pounds per square inch, ink adheres in raised ridges, providing security and tactile accessibility.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Intaglio printing)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 15, 'question': 'How many raised diagonal bleed lines are present on the left and right margins of an Indian ₹500 banknote for accessibility?', 'options': ['3 bleed lines', '4 bleed lines', '5 bleed lines', '7 bleed lines'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'The ₹500 banknote features 5 raised angular bleed lines on each edge along with a circular tactile identification mark.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Tactile Bleed Line Standards:</strong></p>\n                <ul class='list-disc pl-4 space-y-1 text-slate-300'>\n                    <li><strong>₹100:</strong> 4 bleed lines + triangle symbol</li>\n                    <li><strong>₹200:</strong> 4 bleed lines with 2 circles in between + round symbol</li>\n                    <li><strong>₹500:</strong> 5 bleed lines + circle symbol</li>\n                </ul>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: C (5 bleed lines)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 16, 'question': 'What does the abbreviation MANI stand for in the assistive mobile application launched by the Reserve Bank of India?', 'options': ['Money And National Income', 'Mobile Aided Note Identifier', 'Micro Asset Network Interface', 'Mutual Account Net Identification'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'MANI stands for Mobile Aided Note Identifier, developed to assist visually challenged individuals in recognizing banknote denominations.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>MANI App Functions:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>The app uses computer vision machine learning on a smartphone to detect currency notes under varied lighting conditions and speaks the denomination in Hindi or English, completely offline.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Mobile Aided Note Identifier)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 17, 'question': 'How many regional languages are printed within the language panel on the reverse of an Indian rupee banknote?', 'options': ['10 languages', '12 languages', '15 languages', '22 languages'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'The central language panel on the reverse side features 15 regional languages in alphabetical order.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Language Panel Breakdown:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>The front displays Hindi and English prominently. The reverse panel contains 15 languages: Assamese, Bengali, Gujarati, Kannada, Kashmiri, Konkani, Malayalam, Marathi, Nepali, Odia, Punjabi, Sanskrit, Tamil, Telugu, and Urdu (total 17 linguistic representations on each note).</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: C (15 languages)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 18, 'question': 'Which historic monument is depicted on the reverse side of the Indian ₹500 note?', 'options': ['Taj Mahal, Agra', 'Red Fort, New Delhi', 'Gateway of India, Mumbai', 'Qutub Minar, Delhi'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The reverse of the stone-grey ₹500 banknote depicts the Red Fort in Delhi with the Indian Tricolour flag flying above its ramparts.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>The Red Fort Symbolism:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Built by Mughal Emperor Shah Jahan in 1638, the Red Fort serves as the historic venue from where the Prime Minister addresses the nation and hoists the flag on Independence Day.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Red Fort, New Delhi)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 19, 'question': 'How does physical banknote dimension differ across denominations from ₹10 up to ₹500?', 'options': ['All notes have identical height and width', 'Notes decrease in size as their value increases', 'Notes progressively increase in length and width as denomination value increases', 'Size changes randomly every year'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Higher denomination banknotes are physically wider and longer, allowing visually impaired people to sort notes by tactile size comparison.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Progressive Dimensional Sizing:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>A ₹10 note measures 123 mm × 63 mm, while a ₹500 note measures 150 mm × 66 mm. This systematic gradient aids rapid physical sorting.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: C</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 20, 'question': 'What is the green-to-blue colour-shifting security thread embedded vertically into higher-denomination banknotes called?', 'options': ['Cotton ribbon', 'Optically Variable Security Thread', 'Plastic bookmark', 'Magnetic tape'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The windowed security thread contains colour-shifting ink that transitions from green to blue when the note is tilted in light.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Security Thread Physics:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>The thread carries micro-inscriptions reading 'RBI' and 'भारत'. When tilted, microscopic optical coatings interfere with light wavelengths, shifting perceived colour from green to deep blue to thwart photocopiers.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Optically Variable Security Thread)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 21, 'question': 'From which ancient archaeological sculpture was the State Emblem of India adapted?', 'options': ['Iron Pillar of Delhi', 'Sarnath Lion Capital of Emperor Ashoka', 'Ajanta Cave Frescoes', 'Brihadisvara Temple sculptures'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The National Emblem is an adaptation of the 3rd century BCE polished sandstone Lion Capital erected by Emperor Ashoka at Sarnath.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Historical Heritage of the Capital:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Erected around 250 BCE at Sarnath, where Lord Buddha gave his first sermon (Dharmachakra Pravartana), the sculpture represents spiritual authority, peace, and universal dharma.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Sarnath Lion Capital of Emperor Ashoka)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 22, 'question': 'How many Asiatic lions stand carved back-to-back on the Lion Capital at Sarnath?', 'options': ['2 lions', '3 lions', '4 lions', '8 lions'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'There are four Asiatic lions standing back-to-back facing the four cardinal directions, although only three can be seen simultaneously in a 2D drawing.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Three-Dimensional Sculpture Design:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>The sculpture has 4 lions guarding north, south, east, and west. In official 2D paper seals, three lions are visible from any single viewing angle, while the fourth remains hidden behind.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: C (4 lions)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 23, 'question': "Which two animals flank the central Ashoka Chakra on the circular abacus of India's National Emblem?", 'options': ['A tiger on the left and an elephant on the right', 'A galloping horse on the left and a humped bull on the right', 'A deer on the left and a peacock on the right', 'A camel on the left and a rhinoceros on the right'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The circular abacus features a galloping horse on the left (representing speed and energy) and a bull on the right (representing steadfast labour).', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Animals on the Abacus:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Between the wheels, the abacus depicts four animals in relief: a lion, an elephant, a galloping horse, and a humped zebu bull. In the front view, the horse and bull flank the wheel.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (A galloping horse on the left and a humped bull on the right)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 24, 'question': "From which sacred ancient text is the national motto 'Satyameva Jayate' taken?", 'options': ['Bhagavad Gita', 'Mundaka Upanishad', 'Rigveda', 'Panchatantra'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': "'Satyameva Jayate' is an aphorism from the Mundaka Upanishad, proclaiming that truth alone triumphs.", 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Upanishadic Origin:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Mundaka Upanishad (3.1.6) states: 'Satyameva Jayate Nanritam'—Truth alone triumphs, not falsehood. Through truth divine the path is widened.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Mundaka Upanishad)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 25, 'question': "Which state of India is the exclusive home to the world's only wild population of Asiatic lions?", 'options': ['Rajasthan', 'Gujarat (Gir Forest)', 'Madhya Pradesh', 'Karnataka'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The Gir National Park and surrounding sanctuary in Gujarat is the only wild refuge for the Asiatic Lion on Earth.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Gir Conservation Triumph:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Through strict protection by the Gujarat forest department and local Maldhari pastoralist communities, Asiatic lion numbers have grown from fewer than 20 in 1913 to over 674 lions today.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Gujarat - Gir Forest)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 26, 'question': 'In which year was the Royal Bengal Tiger declared the National Animal of India under Project Tiger?', 'options': ['1947', '1950', '1973', '2000'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': "The tiger replaced the lion as India's National Animal in 1973, when Project Tiger was inaugurated at Corbett National Park.", 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Project Tiger Milestone:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Prior to 1973, the lion was considered the national animal. Project Tiger was launched in April 1973 to save Bengal tigers from extinction caused by rampant colonial trophy hunting and habitat loss.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: C (1973)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 27, 'question': "Approximately what percentage of the entire world's wild tiger population lives in India today?", 'options': ['10%', '25%', '50%', 'More than 75%'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'India houses more than 75% of all wild tigers globally, with the 2023 census estimating over 3,682 wild tigers.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Global Tiger Census Data:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Across 55 dedicated tiger reserves spanning over 78,000 square kilometres, India has successfully doubled its tiger count well ahead of global conservation targets.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: D (More than 75%)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 28, 'question': 'What is the scientific name of the Indian Peafowl, the National Bird of India?', 'options': ['Panthera tigris', 'Pavo cristatus', 'Nelumbo nucifera', 'Ficus benghalensis'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The scientific binomial name of the Indian Peafowl is Pavo cristatus.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Taxonomic Identification:</strong></p>\n                <ul class='list-disc pl-4 space-y-1 text-slate-300'>\n                    <li><strong>Pavo cristatus:</strong> Indian Peafowl (National Bird)</li>\n                    <li><strong>Panthera tigris:</strong> Bengal Tiger (National Animal)</li>\n                    <li><strong>Nelumbo nucifera:</strong> Indian Lotus (National Flower)</li>\n                    <li><strong>Ficus benghalensis:</strong> Banyan Tree (National Tree)</li>\n                </ul>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Pavo cristatus)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 29, 'question': 'What sensory technique does the blind Ganges River Dolphin use to locate food and navigate muddy river silt?', 'options': ['Infrared heat sensing', 'Ultrasonic echolocation clicks', 'Magnetic field compass', 'Taste sensors on its fins'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The Ganges River Dolphin emits high-frequency ultrasonic clicks and listens to echoes bouncing off objects, navigating flawlessly without eyesight.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Acoustic Biosonar in Murky Water:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>In turbid river waters of the Ganga and Brahmaputra, optical vision is useless. The dolphin uses a specialized fatty organ called a 'melon' on its head to focus sound beam clicks for echolocation.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Ultrasonic echolocation clicks)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 30, 'question': 'What botanical structure allows a Banyan tree (Ficus benghalensis) to support enormous horizontal limbs and spread across acres?', 'options': ['Floating water bulbs', 'Thick woody aerial prop roots', 'Thorny creeping tendrils', 'Hollow underground bamboo shoots'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Aerial prop roots drop from horizontal branches into the ground, thickening into secondary supporting trunks.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Prop Root Biomechanics:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>As branches grow heavy and extend horizontally, aerial roots descend from them. Once anchored in soil, they absorb nutrients and thicken into stout columnar pillars, supporting immense canopies.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Thick woody aerial prop roots)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 31, 'question': 'From which ancient mother script did almost all modern Indian writing scripts (including Devanagari, Bengali, and Tamil) evolve?', 'options': ['Cuneiform', 'Brahmi script', 'Phoenician', 'Hieratic'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The ancient Brahmi script, dating back over 2,300 years, is the historical ancestor of the Brahmic family of Indian scripts.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Script Lineage of India:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Emperor Ashoka used Brahmi for his edicts. Over centuries, northern variants developed into Gupta script, Nagari, and Devanagari, while southern variants evolved into Grantha, Tamil, Telugu, and Kannada scripts.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Brahmi script)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 32, 'question': 'Why is a diverse natural forest resilient to pests and droughts, whereas a single-crop garden collapses easily?', 'options': ['A single-crop garden has no soil', 'A diverse forest ecosystem features multiple complementary species that balance pests, share nutrients, and prevent single-point disease failure', 'Forest animals carry water in buckets to trees', 'Single-crop gardens never receive sunlight'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Ecosystem biodiversity provides redundancy and ecological stability: diverse root depths, multiple food chains, and natural predator balance protect the forest.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Biodiversity & Stability Principle:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Monocultures are vulnerable because all plants share identical genetic weaknesses. A biodiverse forest interweaves different root layers, mycorrhizal networks, and natural predators that prevent pest outbreaks.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 33, 'question': 'What is the primary function of the multi-layered cotton Saafa or Pagri worn by people in Rajasthan?', 'options': ['To hold keys and coins inside', 'To insulate the head from scorching desert solar radiation by trapping dead air in cotton folds', 'To make the wearer look taller than horses', 'To protect the ears from cold snow'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The multiple loose folds of lightweight cotton trap dead air, which is a poor conductor of heat, shielding the skull from extreme desert temperatures.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Thermal Physics of Desert Turbans:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Air is one of nature's best thermal insulators when trapped in small spaces. The saafa prevents direct radiant sunbeams from heating the cranial bone and evaporating body moisture.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 34, 'question': 'What acoustic feature on the membrane of an Indian Tabla or Mridangam enables it to produce harmonically tuned pitch?', 'options': ['A small hole drilled in the wooden shell', 'The Syahi (or Karanai), a black loaded circular paste of iron filings and starch in the centre', 'Thick metal strings tied across the leather head', 'A coat of waterproof varnish'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The central black Syahi paste adds localized mass to the membrane, suppressing discordant non-harmonic frequencies and generating pure musical harmonics.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Acoustic Tuning by Mass Loading:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Simple circular drum skins vibrate in Bessel modes with non-integer overtone ratios (sounding like noise). The Syahi's parabolic mass distribution shifts overtones into harmonic ratios (1:2:3:4), creating musical notes.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 35, 'question': 'Which Indian classical dance style from Kerala is renowned for elaborate facial face-paint (chutti) and dramatic ocular (eye) storytelling?', 'options': ['Bharatanatyam', 'Kathakali', 'Bhangra', 'Garba'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Kathakali is the celebrated classical dance-drama of Kerala famous for its stylized makeup, gigantic headdresses, and intricate eye movements.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Kathakali Characteristics:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Green face paint (Paccha) represents noble heroes; red (Katti) represents fierce characters. Actors undergo years of rigorous eye exercises to express emotions without spoken dialogue.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Kathakali)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 36, 'question': 'What are codified symbolic hand and finger gestures used in Indian classical dance and drama called?', 'options': ['Asanas', 'Hastas or Mudras', 'Chakkars', 'Ragas'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Mudras (or Hastas) are symbolic finger and hand gestures used to depict natural objects, characters, and actions.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Sign Language of Classical Dance:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>From the Sanskrit root 'Mud' (delight), Mudras act as a visual vocabulary. Dancers form specific hand shapes to portray flowers, water currents, peacocks, elephants, and philosophical concepts.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Hastas or Mudras)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 37, 'question': 'Which active chemical compound in golden turmeric (Haldi) provides scientifically proven anti-inflammatory and antiseptic effects?', 'options': ['Quinine', 'Curcumin', 'Ascorbic acid', 'Acetic acid'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Curcumin is the primary bioactive polyphenol in turmeric responsible for suppressing inflammatory enzymes and accelerating tissue healing.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Curcumin Phytochemistry:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Centuries of Ayurvedic use of Haridra (turmeric) are corroborated by modern clinical trials proving curcumin neutralizes free radicals and inhibits NF-kB inflammatory pathways.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Curcumin)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 38, 'question': "What historic achievement made ISRO's Chandrayaan-3 mission in 2023 celebrated across the entire world?", 'options': ['It carried the first astronaut to Mars', "It achieved the world's first successful soft landing near the lunar South Pole", 'It orbited the Sun closer than any probe', 'It discovered a new ring around Saturn'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': "On August 23, 2023, India's Chandrayaan-3 became the first spacecraft to touch down on the rugged lunar south polar region.", 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Lunar South Pole Milestone:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>The lunar south pole contains permanently shadowed craters harbouring deep water-ice deposits. India's Vikram lander and Pragyan rover conducted in-situ soil measurements, confirming the presence of sulphur and oxygen.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 39, 'question': 'What digital payment technology developed in India allows instant, direct bank-to-bank transactions via mobile QR codes?', 'options': ['SwiftWire', 'Unified Payments Interface (UPI)', 'BitToken Network', 'CardSwipe Direct'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'UPI (Unified Payments Interface), launched by NPCI, powers real-time, interoperable mobile payments across millions of merchants and citizens.', 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Digital Public Infrastructure:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>UPI integrates multiple bank accounts into a single mobile application, merging seamless fund routing and merchant payments without requiring credit card machines or exposing bank account numbers.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B (Unified Payments Interface - UPI)</p>\n            </div>\n        "}, {'id': 40, 'question': "What core message does the philosophy of 'Unity in Diversity' convey about the Indian nation?", 'options': ['All people must forget their regional cultures and wear identical clothes', 'That our diverse languages, arts, cuisines, and traditions strengthen and enrich our united national fabric rather than dividing it', 'That only one festival should be celebrated each year', 'That people should stop traveling between states'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': "'Unity in Diversity' celebrates how India's rich pluralism forms a resilient, harmonious, and indivisible national identity.", 'solution_html': "\n            <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                <p><strong>Grand Synthesis of Indian Unity:</strong></p>\n                <p class='text-slate-300'>Just as different threads in a tapestry or different species in a pristine forest create an unbreakable, beautiful whole, India's multiplicity of traditions, combined with constitutional brotherhood, forms the proud backbone of our vibrant nation.</p>\n                <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: B</p>\n            </div>\n        "}]
